A Bounded Context has its own **Ubiquitous Language** — a vocabulary agreed between developers and domain experts that lives directly in the code:

```java
// in Sales BC
public class Customer {
    public Opportunity qualify(QualificationCriteria criteria) { ... }
    public LeadScore currentLeadScore() { ... }
}

// in Support BC
public class Customer {
    public Ticket escalate(EscalationReason reason) { ... }
    public SlaTier slaTier() { ... }
}
```

The verbs (`qualify`, `escalate`) and nouns (`Opportunity`, `Ticket`) are different because the **language is different**. When you find that the same word needs a different vocabulary to describe its behavior in two places, **you've crossed a context boundary**.

**Practical rule:** if you and a domain expert disagree about what a word means, ask which context they're in. If the answer is 'both' — you've found a boundary.

```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
    public Money {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount);
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency);
        if (amount.scale() > currency.getDefaultFractionDigits()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Too many decimal places for " + currency);
        }
    }

    public Money plus(Money other) {
        if (!currency.equals(other.currency)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Cannot add " + currency + " and " + other.currency);
        }
        return new Money(amount.add(other.amount), currency);
    }

    public Money times(int qty) {
        return new Money(amount.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(qty)), currency);
    }

    public boolean isGreaterThan(Money other) {
        return amount.compareTo(other.amount) > 0;
    }
}
```

Key traits:
- **Records auto-generate** `equals`, `hashCode`, `toString` based on components — attribute-based equality is automatic.
- **Compact constructor** validates invariants once, at construction.
- **No setters.** All operations return a *new* `Money`. The currency-mismatch check inside `plus` makes invalid arithmetic impossible by design.
- **Behavior on the type.** `money.plus(other)` reads better than `MoneyService.add(money, other)` and lives where it belongs.

```java
public class Order {
    private final OrderId id;             // identity
    private final CustomerId customerId;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money total;

    private Order(OrderId id, CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order needs items");
        this.id = id;
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.items = new ArrayList<>(items);
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        this.total = computeTotal(items);
    }

    public static Order create(CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        return new Order(OrderId.next(), customerId, items);
    }

    public void cancel() {
        if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Order other && id.equals(other.id);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() { return id.hashCode(); }
}
```

Key traits:
- **Identity-based equality.** Two `Order`s with identical items are different orders if their IDs differ.
- **Controlled mutation.** State changes through named business methods (`cancel()`), never raw setters.
- **`OrderId` is itself a value object** — strongly typed; you can't pass a `CustomerId` where an `OrderId` is expected.

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id
    private String id;

    @Column(name = "customer_id")
    private String customerId;

    @Embedded
    @AttributeOverrides({
        @AttributeOverride(name = "amount",   column = @Column(name = "total_amount")),
        @AttributeOverride(name = "currency", column = @Column(name = "total_currency"))
    })
    private MoneyEmbeddable total;

    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "order_items", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "order_id"))
    private List<OrderItemEmbeddable> items;
}

@Embeddable
public class MoneyEmbeddable {
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String currency;
    protected MoneyEmbeddable() { } // JPA
}
```

- `Order` is `@Entity` — owns its row, has a primary key.
- `Money` is `@Embeddable` — its columns live **inside** the order's row (`total_amount`, `total_currency`). No separate table, no join.
- Same for `Address`, `DateRange`, etc.

**Wrong:**
```java
@Entity                         // ❌ Money is not an entity
@Table(name = "money")
public class Money {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    BigDecimal amount;
    String currency;
}
```
Now every order has a foreign key to a `money` table; every order line item creates two extra rows; queries explode in joins. None of it represents real domain identity.

```java
public class Order {  // aggregate root
    private final OrderId id;
    private final CustomerId customerId;       // by ID, not Customer object
    private final List<OrderItem> items;       // internal entities
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money total;
    private long version;                      // for optimistic concurrency

    private Order(OrderId id, CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order needs items");
        this.id = id;
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.items = new ArrayList<>(items);
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        this.total = computeTotal(items);
    }

    public static Order create(CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        return new Order(OrderId.next(), customerId, items);
    }

    public void addItem(ProductId productId, int qty, Money unitPrice) {
        if (status != OrderStatus.PENDING) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot modify after submission");
        }
        items.add(new OrderItem(productId, qty, unitPrice));
        this.total = computeTotal(items);   // invariant maintained
    }

    public void submit() {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("Empty order");
        this.status = OrderStatus.SUBMITTED;
    }

    public List<OrderItem> items() {
        return List.copyOf(items);   // defensive copy; no aliasing
    }

    private static Money computeTotal(List<OrderItem> items) {
        return items.stream()
            .map(i -> i.unitPrice().times(i.qty()))
            .reduce(Money.zero("USD"), Money::plus);
    }
}
```

Key rules in this code:
- `OrderItem` is created **only** through `Order.addItem(...)` — no external `new OrderItem(...)` reachable from outside.
- `Order.total` is recomputed inside `addItem` — the invariant 'total = sum of items' is **structurally impossible to violate**.
- Returns `List.copyOf(items)` — callers can't mutate the internal list.

**Rule 1: Reference other aggregates by ID, not by object.**
```java
// ✅ right
class Order {
    private CustomerId customerId;
}

// ❌ wrong
class Order {
    private Customer customer;   // pulls entire Customer aggregate
}
```
Referencing by object means loading the other aggregate too. JPA lazy-loads it; the moment you access a field it materializes. Hidden coupling, hidden joins, hidden contention.

**Rule 2: One transaction, one aggregate.**
Multi-aggregate updates happen across multiple transactions, coordinated via **domain events** and **eventual consistency**.
```java
// ❌ wrong: two aggregates in one transaction
@Transactional
void placeOrder() {
    customer.recordOrder(order);  // changes Customer aggregate
    orderRepo.save(order);        // changes Order aggregate
}

// ✅ right
@Transactional
void placeOrder() {
    orderRepo.save(order);
    eventPublisher.publish(new OrderPlacedEvent(order.id(), order.customerId()));
}
// Customer aggregate updates in a separate transaction, on event consumption
```

**Rule 3: Aggregates should be small.**
Large aggregates (`Customer` with all orders, addresses, payments) create:
- Contention (every order-placement locks the whole customer).
- Heavy loads (loading 5,000 orders to update one).
- Memory pressure.
A good test: if your aggregate has thousands of internal entities, it's too big.

**Rule 4: All invariants that must hold transactionally live inside one aggregate.**
If 'order total = sum of items' must always be true, items must be inside the order aggregate. If 'a customer can have at most 3 active orders' must always be true — either model that inside the customer aggregate, or relax it to eventually consistent (preferable).

Two concurrent requests both modify the same order. Without protection, the second overwrites the first.

```java
@Entity
class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id String id;
    @Version Long version;        // JPA optimistic lock
    // ...
}
```

- JPA increments `version` on every `UPDATE` and adds `WHERE version = ?` to the WHERE clause.
- Concurrent updates: the second one sees `0 rows updated` → throws `OptimisticLockException`.
- The use case retries (re-read aggregate, re-apply command) or returns `409 Conflict`.

**Why this works at the aggregate level:** the root carries the version. Updating any internal entity bumps the root's version. Concurrency is detected at the consistency-boundary level, not per-row — which matches the design intent.

**One repository per aggregate.** The repository deals with the root only:

```java
public interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id);
    List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(CustomerId customerId);
}
```

**Anti-pattern:** repository per JPA entity:
```java
OrderRepository, OrderItemRepository, ShippingInfoRepository, PaymentMethodRepository
```
This fragments the aggregate, leaks the persistence model upward, and makes the use case orchestrate four saves to persist one logical change.

**Right shape:** `OrderRepository.save(order)` persists the root *and* its internal entities atomically. The persistence adapter handles the multi-table mapping internally.

When a change in one aggregate must trigger changes in another:

```java
// Inside Order aggregate
public void submit() {
    if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException();
    this.status = OrderStatus.SUBMITTED;
    this.events.add(new OrderSubmittedEvent(id, customerId, total, Instant.now()));
}

// Use case publishes events after persistence
@Transactional
public void placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
    Order order = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), cmd.items());
    order.submit();
    orderRepo.save(order);
    domainEvents.publishAll(order.drainEvents());
}

// Other aggregate consumes the event in its own transaction
class CustomerEventHandler {
    @EventListener
    @Transactional
    public void on(OrderSubmittedEvent e) {
        Customer c = customerRepo.findById(e.customerId()).orElseThrow();
        c.recordOrder(e.orderId(), e.total());
        customerRepo.save(c);
    }
}
```

- Order aggregate update: transaction 1.
- Customer aggregate update: transaction 2.
- Eventually consistent. Brief window where Order is submitted but Customer hasn't recorded it.
- For reliable delivery: **transactional outbox** — events written to an outbox table in the same transaction; a background process publishes from the outbox to Kafka.

```java
public class Order {  // aggregate root
    private final OrderId id;
    private final CustomerId customerId;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money total;

    private final List<DomainEvent> events = new ArrayList<>();

    public static Order create(CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        Order o = new Order(OrderId.next(), customerId, items);
        o.events.add(new OrderPlacedEvent(
            o.id, o.customerId, o.total, Instant.now()));
        return o;
    }

    public void cancel(String reason) {
        if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
        events.add(new OrderCancelledEvent(id, reason, Instant.now()));
    }

    public List<DomainEvent> drainEvents() {
        var out = List.copyOf(events);
        events.clear();
        return out;
    }
}
```

Notice:
- The aggregate just **records** events on its internal list — it doesn't publish them.
- The application layer drains and publishes after persistence (next section).
- This decoupling lets aggregates stay framework-free and unit-testable.

```java
public interface DomainEvent {
    Instant occurredAt();
}

public record OrderPlacedEvent(
    OrderId orderId,
    CustomerId customerId,
    Money total,
    Instant occurredAt
) implements DomainEvent {}

public record OrderCancelledEvent(
    OrderId orderId,
    String reason,
    Instant occurredAt
) implements DomainEvent {}
```

Guidelines:
- **Records** are perfect: immutable, value-equality, succinct.
- **Past tense** in the name.
- **Carry only what subscribers need** — IDs (so subscribers can look up if they need more), key value objects (`Money`, `Address`), the timestamp.
- **Avoid carrying entire aggregates** — events are facts, not snapshots.

**Wrong:** publish inside the transaction.
```java
@Transactional
public void placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
    Order o = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), cmd.items());
    publisher.publish(new OrderPlacedEvent(o.id(), ...));   // ⚠
    orderRepo.save(o);
}
```
If the consumer is fast and synchronous, it might query for the order — and not find it (transaction not committed yet). If the transaction later rolls back, you've published a phantom event.

**Right:** publish after commit.
```java
@Transactional
public Order placeOrder(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
    Order o = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), cmd.items());
    orderRepo.save(o);
    return o;   // events drained outside this method
}

// At the use-case-aspect or controller layer:
Order o = placeOrder.execute(cmd);
publisher.publishAll(o.drainEvents());   // after commit
```

Or use Spring's `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)` so listeners only fire when the transaction has committed.

```java
@EventListener
@TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
public void on(OrderPlacedEvent e) { ... }
```

**In-process (one Spring app):** `ApplicationEventPublisher` is enough.

```java
@Component
class OrderPlacedHandler {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
        // send confirmation email, update read model, etc.
    }
}
```

- Synchronous (same thread by default) or async (`@Async`).
- Listener exceptions don't roll back the producer's transaction.
- Good for **same-bounded-context** reactions.

**Cross-service:** publish to Kafka / RabbitMQ. **You hit the dual-write problem.**

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    UC[Use Case] --> DB[(Postgres<br/>save order)]
    UC --> Kafka[(Kafka<br/>publish event)]
```

What if DB save succeeds but Kafka publish fails (or vice versa)? You have inconsistent state across systems.

**The fix:** transactional outbox.

Kafka and most message brokers guarantee **at-least-once** delivery. Your consumer **will** see the same event twice eventually.

Make consumers **idempotent**:

```java
@KafkaListener(topics = "order-events")
@Transactional
public void on(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
    if (processedEventRepo.existsById(e.eventId())) return;   // dedupe
    // ... do the work ...
    processedEventRepo.save(new ProcessedEvent(e.eventId(), Instant.now()));
}
```

Or design the side effect so duplicate application is harmless:
- Sending an email → dedupe by event ID.
- Updating a read model with `UPSERT` keyed on aggregate ID — naturally idempotent.
- Creating a new entity — use the event ID as the natural key.

**Never assume exactly-once delivery** without specific guarantees from the broker and your consumer.

```java
// domain layer
public interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id);
    Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key);
    List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(CustomerId customerId);
}

// adapter layer
@Entity @Table(name = "orders")
class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id String id;
    @Version Long version;
    String customerId;
    String idempotencyKey;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) OrderStatus status;
    @Embedded MoneyEmbeddable total;
    @ElementCollection List<OrderItemEmbeddable> items;
    Instant createdAt;
    protected OrderJpaEntity() {}
}

interface OrderSpringRepo extends JpaRepository<OrderJpaEntity, String> {
    Optional<OrderJpaEntity> findByIdempotencyKey(String key);
    List<OrderJpaEntity> findByCustomerIdAndStatusIn(String customerId, Set<OrderStatus> statuses);
}

@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderSpringRepo jpa;
    private final OrderMapper mapper;

    public void save(Order order) {
        jpa.save(mapper.toJpa(order));   // root + items in one save
    }

    public Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id) {
        return jpa.findById(id.value()).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }

    public Optional<Order> findByIdempotencyKey(String key) {
        return jpa.findByIdempotencyKey(key).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }

    public List<Order> findActiveByCustomerId(CustomerId customerId) {
        return jpa.findByCustomerIdAndStatusIn(customerId.value(),
                Set.of(OrderStatus.PENDING, OrderStatus.CONFIRMED))
            .stream().map(mapper::toDomain).toList();
    }
}
```

Use cases see only `OrderRepository`. JPA is invisible above the adapter.

Repositories are great for fetching aggregates by domain criteria. They become awkward for:

- Reporting / analytics queries (aggregate sums, group-bys).
- Complex search across many fields.
- Projection DTOs for UI screens that don't match the aggregate shape.

**Two patterns help:**

**1. Specification pattern** — build query criteria as composable objects, hand them to the repository:
```java
public interface Specification<T> { boolean matches(T item); /* or build a JPA predicate */ }

repo.findAll(activeOrdersFor(customerId).and(placedSince(now.minusDays(30))));
```
Useful for moderately complex queries that still return aggregates.

**2. CQRS-style query services** — a separate interface returning DTOs:
```java
public interface OrderQueryService {
    OrderSummaryDto findSummary(OrderId id);
    Page<OrderListDto> search(OrderSearchCriteria criteria, Pageable page);
    OrderStatsDto monthlyStats(YearMonth month);
}
```
- Returns DTOs, not aggregates (queries don't need behavior).
- Implementation can use SQL views, JPA projections, or hit a separate read model.
- Keeps the write-side `OrderRepository` focused on the aggregate's lifecycle.

For read-heavy systems, separating commands from queries (CQRS lite) is the practical move. Don't try to express every query as a domain repository method.

```java
// pure domain, no framework
public class FundsTransferService {
    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, Money amount) {
        if (!from.canDebit(amount)) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException(from.id(), amount);
        }
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
    }
}
```

- No `@Service`, no Spring, no `@Transactional`.
- No repository calls.
- Operates on already-loaded aggregates.
- Pure orchestration of domain methods.

**The application service is what loads / saves:**
```java
public class TransferFundsUseCase {
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;
    private final FundsTransferService transferService;   // domain service

    @Transactional
    public void execute(TransferCommand cmd) {
        Account from = accountRepo.findById(cmd.fromAccountId()).orElseThrow();
        Account to = accountRepo.findById(cmd.toAccountId()).orElseThrow();

        transferService.transfer(from, to, cmd.amount());

        accountRepo.save(from);
        accountRepo.save(to);
    }
}
```

Notice the clean split: the use case handles I/O and transactions; the domain service handles the domain rule.

Application services are **thin**. Their job is plumbing:

1. Pull aggregates via repositories.
2. Delegate to entities / value objects / domain services.
3. Persist the result.
4. Publish domain events.
5. Manage the transaction boundary.

```java
public class PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryGateway inventory;
    private final DomainEventPublisher events;

    @Transactional
    public OrderId execute(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        // 1. Pull / construct
        Order order = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), cmd.items());
        // 2. Domain logic via aggregate / domain services
        inventory.reserve(cmd.items());
        order.submit();
        // 3. Persist
        orderRepo.save(order);
        // 4. Events
        events.publishAll(order.drainEvents());
        return order.id();
    }
}
```

**No business logic in this method.** Every business decision (`cancel`, `submit`, `addItem`) lives in `Order` (or a domain service). The use case is a script: load, decide via domain, save, emit.

When developers default to Spring `@Service`, they pour business logic into application services:

```java
// ❌ anemic domain + fat application service
@Service
class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void cancel(OrderId id) {
        Order o = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        if (o.getStatus() == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) {           // ❌ business rule
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped");
        }
        o.setStatus(OrderStatus.CANCELLED);                    // ❌ invariant violation possible
        orderRepo.save(o);
    }
}
```

Problems:
- Business rule is duplicated in every place that cancels (refund flow, admin flow, scheduled cleanup).
- `Order` is anemic; invalid state reachable via `setStatus` from anywhere.
- Fat service classes accumulate dozens of methods.

**Fix:** push the rule into the entity:
```java
// ✅
class Order {
    public void cancel() {
        if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
        events.add(new OrderCancelledEvent(id, Instant.now()));
    }
}

@Service
class CancelOrderUseCase {
    @Transactional
    public void execute(OrderId id) {
        Order o = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        o.cancel();              // entity decides; use case orchestrates
        orderRepo.save(o);
        events.publishAll(o.drainEvents());
    }
}
```

Ubiquitous Language is not a glossary that lives in a wiki and rots. **It lives in the code:**

```java
// ✅ language matches business
public class Lead {
    public Opportunity qualify(QualificationCriteria criteria) {
        if (!criteria.isMet(this)) throw new LeadNotReadyException();
        return new Opportunity(this, Instant.now());
    }
}

// ❌ language drifts from business
public class Prospect {
    public void updateStatus(String status) { ... }
    public void setSalesPersonId(Long id) { ... }
}
```

- Class name, method name, parameter type, exception name — all express the business concept.
- Reading the code tells you the business rule: 'qualify a lead with these criteria, get an opportunity.'
- A domain expert can read the public API and understand it.

**Tests reinforce the language:**
```java
@Test
void lead_with_met_criteria_qualifies_into_opportunity() { ... }

@Test
void lead_with_unmet_criteria_throws_LeadNotReadyException() { ... }
```

Readable test names *are* domain documentation.

```java
// === your domain layer ===
public interface CrmGateway {
    Optional<Customer> findCustomer(CustomerId id);
    void recordContact(CustomerId id, ContactType type);
}

public record Customer(
    CustomerId id,
    String name,
    EmailAddress email,
    LoyaltyTier tier
) {}

// === ACL adapter (the only code that imports legacy CRM types) ===
@Component
class LegacyCrmGatewayAdapter implements CrmGateway {
    private final LegacyCrmClient legacyClient;
    private final LegacyCustomerTranslator translator;

    @Override
    public Optional<Customer> findCustomer(CustomerId id) {
        try {
            LegacyCustomer foreign = legacyClient.fetchCustomerByIdent(id.value());
            return Optional.ofNullable(foreign).map(translator::toDomain);
        } catch (LegacyCustomerNotFoundException e) {
            return Optional.empty();
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void recordContact(CustomerId id, ContactType type) {
        String legacyType = switch (type) {
            case PHONE  -> "PH-1";
            case EMAIL  -> "EM-NEW";
            case INPERSON -> "IP-V2";
        };
        legacyClient.logTouchpoint(id.value(), legacyType, Instant.now().toString());
    }
}

// translator hides field-level mapping
class LegacyCustomerTranslator {
    Customer toDomain(LegacyCustomer src) {
        return new Customer(
            new CustomerId(src.getCustIdent()),
            src.getFirstNm() + " " + src.getLastNm(),
            new EmailAddress(src.getEmlAddr1()),
            mapTier(src.getTierCd())
        );
    }

    private LoyaltyTier mapTier(String legacyCode) {
        return switch (legacyCode) {
            case "T1-OLD", "T1-NEW" -> LoyaltyTier.GOLD;
            case "T2"               -> LoyaltyTier.SILVER;
            default                  -> LoyaltyTier.BRONZE;
        };
    }
}
```

- Use cases see `CrmGateway` and `Customer` — your language.
- The legacy CRM's `LegacyCustomer`, `LegacyCrmClient`, weird enum codes — contained inside the ACL.

```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, Currency currency) {
    public Money {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount);
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency);
        if (amount.scale() > currency.getDefaultFractionDigits())
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Too many decimal places");
    }
    public Money plus(Money o) {
        if (!currency.equals(o.currency)) throw new IllegalArgumentException();
        return new Money(amount.add(o.amount), currency);
    }
    public static Money zero(String code) {
        return new Money(BigDecimal.ZERO, Currency.getInstance(code));
    }
}

public record OrderId(String value) {
    public OrderId { Objects.requireNonNull(value); }
    public static OrderId next() { return new OrderId(UUID.randomUUID().toString()); }
}
```

No Spring; no JPA. Records give you immutability, `equals`, `hashCode`, `toString` for free. Behavior lives on the type.

```java
public class Order {  // aggregate root, plain Java
    private final OrderId id;
    private final CustomerId customerId;
    private final List<OrderItem> items;
    private OrderStatus status;
    private Money total;
    private final List<DomainEvent> events = new ArrayList<>();

    private Order(OrderId id, CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Order needs items");
        this.id = id;
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.items = new ArrayList<>(items);
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        this.total = computeTotal(items);
    }

    public static Order create(CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItem> items) {
        Order o = new Order(OrderId.next(), customerId, items);
        o.events.add(new OrderPlacedEvent(o.id, o.customerId, o.total, Instant.now()));
        return o;
    }

    public void cancel() {
        if (status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot cancel shipped");
        this.status = OrderStatus.CANCELLED;
        events.add(new OrderCancelledEvent(id, Instant.now()));
    }

    public List<DomainEvent> drainEvents() {
        var out = List.copyOf(events); events.clear(); return out;
    }

    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Order other && id.equals(other.id);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() { return id.hashCode(); }
}
```

Notice: zero Spring imports, zero JPA. The aggregate is pure domain code, unit-testable in milliseconds.

```java
// === application/domain layer ===
public interface OrderRepository {
    void save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id);
}

// === adapter/out/persistence ===
@Entity @Table(name = "orders")
class OrderJpaEntity {
    @Id String id;
    @Version Long version;
    String customerId;
    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) OrderStatus status;
    @Embedded MoneyEmbeddable total;
    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "order_items", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "order_id"))
    List<OrderItemEmbeddable> items;
    Instant createdAt;
    protected OrderJpaEntity() {}
}

@Embeddable
class MoneyEmbeddable {
    BigDecimal amount;
    String currency;
    protected MoneyEmbeddable() {}
}

interface OrderSpringRepo extends JpaRepository<OrderJpaEntity, String> {}

@Repository
class JpaOrderRepository implements OrderRepository {
    private final OrderSpringRepo jpa;
    private final OrderMapper mapper;
    public void save(Order o)    { jpa.save(mapper.toJpa(o)); }
    public Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id) {
        return jpa.findById(id.value()).map(mapper::toDomain);
    }
}
```

- Domain `Order` and `OrderJpaEntity` are **separate types** with a mapper between them.
- `@Version` on the JPA entity gives optimistic locking at the aggregate-root level.
- The use case sees only `OrderRepository`.

```java
// pure interface in application layer
public interface PlaceOrderUseCase {
    OrderId execute(PlaceOrderCommand cmd);
}

public record PlaceOrderCommand(CustomerId customerId, List<OrderItemRequest> items) {}

// implementation — transactional, orchestrates I/O
public class PlaceOrderInteractor implements PlaceOrderUseCase {
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;
    private final InventoryGateway inventory;
    private final ApplicationEventPublisher publisher;

    public PlaceOrderInteractor(OrderRepository repo, InventoryGateway inv, ApplicationEventPublisher p) {
        this.orderRepo = repo; this.inventory = inv; this.publisher = p;
    }

    @Override
    @Transactional
    public OrderId execute(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        inventory.reserve(cmd.items());
        Order o = Order.create(cmd.customerId(), toDomainItems(cmd.items()));
        orderRepo.save(o);
        o.drainEvents().forEach(publisher::publishEvent);
        return o.id();
    }
}

@Configuration
class UseCaseConfig {
    @Bean PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder(OrderRepository r, InventoryGateway i, ApplicationEventPublisher p) {
        return new PlaceOrderInteractor(r, i, p);
    }
}
```

- `@Transactional` lives at the use-case implementation — the natural transaction boundary.
- `ApplicationEventPublisher` is Spring's in-process event bus.

```java
@Component
class OrderPlacedNotifier {
    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void on(OrderPlacedEvent e) {
        // send confirmation email, update read model, etc.
    }
}
```

- `AFTER_COMMIT` ensures the listener runs only after the producer's transaction has actually committed — no phantom events on rollback, no stale reads.
- For cross-service events: implement the **transactional outbox** pattern (see the dedicated Domain Events question). In the same transaction, save the aggregate and insert event rows into an `outbox` table; a separate worker (or Debezium CDC) publishes from the outbox to Kafka.

```sql
CREATE TABLE outbox (
    id UUID PRIMARY KEY,
    aggregate_id VARCHAR(64),
    event_type VARCHAR(64),
    payload JSONB,
    occurred_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
    published_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL
);
```

```java
// pure domain service, no Spring annotations
public class FundsTransferService {
    public void transfer(Account from, Account to, Money amount) {
        if (!from.canDebit(amount)) throw new InsufficientFundsException();
        from.debit(amount);
        to.credit(amount);
    }
}

// wired by @Configuration
@Configuration
class DomainServiceConfig {
    @Bean FundsTransferService fundsTransferService() { return new FundsTransferService(); }
}
```

The domain service is plain Java; Spring just wires it. The use case loads aggregates, hands them to the domain service, then saves.
